IMPORT `cat-domain`
IMPORT `cat-part6-local-laws`

-- Cat Act 2011 (WA) s 79 and Cat Amendment (Local Laws) Bill 2026 -- worked cases.
--
-- The four local laws are the Explanatory Memorandum's own examples, plus one it
-- does not give. Each is run through both arms of s 79, so the Bill's effect is
-- the difference between two answers rather than an assertion about it.

§ `Fixtures`

-- Declared here rather than imported from cat-cases.l4: that module is the
-- Act's own case file and nothing in Part 6 should depend on it.
`the City of Perth` MEANS LocalGovernment WITH `name` IS "City of Perth"
`the City of Fremantle` MEANS LocalGovernment WITH `name` IS "City of Fremantle"

§ `The local laws`

-- Example 2 to the Bill's new s 79(4): "A local law may prohibit a cat from
-- being in a specified park." Specified place, absolute prohibition -- which is
-- what s 79(3)(f) already says. The Bill does nothing for this one.
`the Hyde Park prohibition` MEANS ProposedLocalLaw WITH
    `name`                           IS "cats prohibited in Hyde Park at all times"
    `made by`                        IS `the City of Perth`
    `prohibits cats from places`     IS TRUE
    `the places are specified`       IS TRUE
    `the prohibition is absolute`    IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places` IS FALSE
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS FALSE
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS FALSE
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS FALSE
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS FALSE
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS FALSE
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS FALSE
    `purports to apply outside the district` IS FALSE
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS FALSE

-- Example 1: "A local law may prohibit a cat from being in all public places
-- between the hours of 7 pm and 5 am." Not absolute, and "all public places"
-- specifies nothing. Beyond power as the Act stands; within power as amended.
`the night curfew` MEANS ProposedLocalLaw WITH
    `name`                           IS "cats prohibited from all public places 7 pm to 5 am"
    `made by`                        IS `the City of Perth`
    `prohibits cats from places`     IS TRUE
    `the places are specified`       IS FALSE
    `the prohibition is absolute`    IS FALSE
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places` IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS FALSE
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS TRUE
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS FALSE
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS FALSE
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS FALSE
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS FALSE
    `purports to apply outside the district` IS FALSE
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS FALSE

-- Example 3: confinement to the premises, with the journey to the vet and the
-- contained-or-controlled cat carved out. The containment local law the JSCDL
-- said the Act did not authorise.
`the containment local law, with the vet carve-out` MEANS ProposedLocalLaw WITH
    `name`                           IS "cats confined to premises, except contained or at veterinary premises"
    `made by`                        IS `the City of Fremantle`
    `prohibits cats from places`     IS TRUE
    `the places are specified`       IS FALSE
    `the prohibition is absolute`    IS FALSE
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places` IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS TRUE
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS FALSE
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS TRUE
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS TRUE
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS TRUE
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS FALSE
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS FALSE
    `purports to apply outside the district` IS FALSE
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS FALSE

-- The same law with Example 3's carve-out dropped. The Bill authorises it, and
-- nothing in the Bill requires the carve-out -- an example is not a requirement.
-- It is the Interpretation Act, not the Cat Act, that answers it.
`the containment local law, no vet carve-out` MEANS ProposedLocalLaw WITH
    `name`                           IS "cats confined to premises, no exception for veterinary premises"
    `made by`                        IS `the City of Fremantle`
    `prohibits cats from places`     IS TRUE
    `the places are specified`       IS FALSE
    `the prohibition is absolute`    IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places` IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS TRUE
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS FALSE
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS FALSE
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS FALSE
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS FALSE
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS FALSE
    `purports to apply outside the district` IS FALSE
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS FALSE

-- The curfew again, this time where the local government establishes that
-- s 79(1) supports it on its own -- "necessary or convenient ... for it to
-- perform any of its functions under this Act", which s 79(3) does not limit.
-- Then the Bill changes nothing, because the law was already within power.
`the night curfew, resting on section 79(1)` MEANS ProposedLocalLaw WITH
    `name`                           IS "cats prohibited from all public places 7 pm to 5 am, under s 79(1)"
    `made by`                        IS `the City of Perth`
    `prohibits cats from places`     IS TRUE
    `the places are specified`       IS FALSE
    `the prohibition is absolute`    IS FALSE
    `prohibits cats from any or all public places` IS TRUE
    `prohibits cats from any place other than where the cat is ordinarily kept` IS FALSE
    `limits the prohibition by time of day or year or by event` IS TRUE
    `excepts a place within the otherwise prohibited area` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats under effective control or contained` IS FALSE
    `defines effective control or contained for the local law` IS FALSE
    `excepts cats being transported to or at veterinary premises` IS FALSE
    `section 79(1) supports it independently of subsection (3)` IS TRUE
    `a paragraph of section 79(3) other than (f) authorises it` IS FALSE
    `purports to apply outside the district` IS FALSE
    `made to apply outside the district under section 80` IS FALSE

§ `What the Bill does, and does not, change`

-- The park prohibition: within power under both. Already authorised, so the
-- amendment is not what makes it good.
#ASSERT `section 79(3)(f) as in force authorises the local law` `the Hyde Park prohibition`
#ASSERT `section 79(3)(f) as amended by Bill 53 authorises the local law` `the Hyde Park prohibition`
#ASSERT NOT `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local law` `the Hyde Park prohibition`

-- The curfew: the headline. Beyond power now, within power as amended.
#ASSERT NOT `section 79(3)(f) as in force authorises the local law` `the night curfew`
#ASSERT `section 79(3)(f) as amended by Bill 53 authorises the local law` `the night curfew`
#ASSERT `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local law` `the night curfew`

-- The containment law: the same, and this is the kind of law the JSCDL
-- considered the Act did not permit.
#ASSERT NOT `a head of power in section 79 as in force supports the local law` `the containment local law, with the vet carve-out`
#ASSERT `a head of power in section 79 as amended by Bill 53 supports the local law` `the containment local law, with the vet carve-out`
#ASSERT `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local law` `the containment local law, with the vet carve-out`

-- s 79(4) is inclusive and confers nothing, so being "of a kind section 79(4)
-- names" is not what makes a law valid. The park prohibition is valid and is of
-- no kind (4) names; the curfew is of a kind it names.
#ASSERT NOT `the local law is of a kind section 79(4) names` `the Hyde Park prohibition`
#ASSERT `the local law is of a kind section 79(4) names` `the night curfew`

-- Where s 79(1) already supports the law, the amendment does no work. The
-- corollary is the one worth reading: the Bill was thought necessary, so the
-- view taken of s 79(1) must have been that it did NOT support containment --
-- otherwise there was nothing to fix. That inference is recorded in INCIDENTS.md
-- (B-02), not asserted here, because no provision makes it true.
#ASSERT `a head of power in section 79 as in force supports the local law` `the night curfew, resting on section 79(1)`
#ASSERT NOT `Bill 53 changes the answer for the local law` `the night curfew, resting on section 79(1)`

§ `Where Part 6 meets the Part 2 duties`

-- The carve-out version leaves the owner able to get the cat to a vet, so the
-- Part 2 duties in ss 14(1) and 18(1) remain performable.
#ASSERT NOT `the local law obstructs compliance with a Part 2 duty` `the containment local law, with the vet carve-out`

-- Without it, the local law forbids the only journey by which s 18(1) --
-- "sterilised by a veterinarian" -- can be complied with. The Cat Act says
-- nothing about this; Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) s 43(1) does, and the local
-- law is void to the extent of the inconsistency.
#ASSERT `the local law obstructs compliance with a Part 2 duty` `the containment local law, no vet carve-out`
#ASSERT `the local law is void to the extent it obstructs a Part 2 duty` `the containment local law, no vet carve-out`

-- And the law is within the amended power notwithstanding, which is the point:
-- validity under s 79 and voidness under s 43(1) are different questions, and a
-- local law can pass the first and fail the second.
#ASSERT `a head of power in section 79 as amended by Bill 53 supports the local law` `the containment local law, no vet carve-out`
